When `set1[0].Priority() == set2[0].Priority()`, calling `compareVictimStructuralImportance` inside a single `for i := range minLen` loop resolves at `i = 0` using `set[0]`'s type (`Pod < PodGroup < CompositePodGroup`) and group size before checking the priorities or pod counts of `set[1..k]`. As a result:
1. A node with `[Pod(prio=20), Pod(prio=19)]` beats `[PodGroup(prio=20, size=2), Pod(prio=1)]` at `i = 0` (`Pod < PodGroup`), evicting a priority-19 pod instead of a priority-1 pod.
2. A node with 10 standalone priority-10 pods beats a node with one 2-pod priority-10 `PodGroup` at `i = 0`, evicting 10 pods instead of 2.
3. A node with three 3-pod priority-10 `PodGroup`s (9 pods) beats a node with one 4-pod priority-10 `PodGroup` (4 pods) at `i = 0` (`3 < 4`).

We can fix this cleanly by comparing priorities across the set first, then total evicted pods across the set, and using `victimRank` / slice length as tie-breakers:
```go
	minLen := min(len(set1), len(set2))
	for i := range minLen {
		vi1, ok1 := set1[i].(Victim)
		vi2, ok2 := set2[i].(Victim)
		if ok1 && ok2 {
			if c := cmp.Compare(vi1.Priority(), vi2.Priority()); c != 0 {
				return c
			}
		}
	}

	var pods1, pods2 int
	for _, v := range set1 {
		pods1 += len(v.Pods())
	}
	for _, v := range set2 {
		pods2 += len(v.Pods())
	}
	if c := cmp.Compare(pods1, pods2); c != 0 {
		return c
	}

	for i := range minLen {
		vi1, ok1 := set1[i].(Victim)
		vi2, ok2 := set2[i].(Victim)
		if ok1 && ok2 {
			if c := cmp.Compare(victimRank(vi1), victimRank(vi2)); c != 0 {
				return c
			}
			if c := cmp.Compare(len(vi1.Pods()), len(vi2.Pods())); c != 0 {
				return c
			}
		}
	}

	return cmp.Compare(len(set1), len(set2))
```

